<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Move Around – Lesson # 4</h1>
        <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
    </div>

    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Let's Dance!</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Move Around</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Specific Objective:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: productive: Label drawings of basic objects. interactive: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <b>Learning Outcome:</b>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Writing: Can draw and label simple movements in pictures (e.g., drawing a person jumping and writing 'jump'). Can engage in interactive labeling activities by matching labels to pictures of simple movements (e.g., 'run' for a picture of running). Can trace letters and simple movement-related words (e.g., 'hop' or 'run').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
    <table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
        <thead>
            <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 40%;">Stage & Description</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 40%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
                <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; text-align: left; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a quick 'Dance Command Simon Says' game. Students stand up. Teacher calls out commands like "Simon says, 'hop!'" or "Simon says, 'spin around!'" (verbs: hop, spin, around). The teacher then shows large flashcards with simple drawings of these actions and the corresponding words (e.g., a child hopping with the word 'hop' below it). Students identify the action and hear the word.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with the word 'jump' (recognizing 'J' in 'jump') and demonstrates how to trace the word in the air with their finger. Then, the teacher uses a large whiteboard or projected worksheet to clearly demonstrate tracing the word 'jump' (and then 'run', 'step') on a line, emphasizing the direction of the letters. The teacher also models drawing a simple stick figure performing the action next to the traced word.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher points to a traced word. "What is this word? Is it 'hop' or 'jump'?" (Students point or say the word). "Can you show me how to trace the letter 'J'?" (Students make the 'J' shape in the air).</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Let's Dance!".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful illustrated cards depicting simple dance commands and actions (e.g., "First, take one step to the side.", "Next, hop over the line.", "Turn around.") with each action word (step, hop, turn, around, line) clearly printed below the illustration. A short animated video or story about dancing children following simple commands.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The learner can understand simple written words and short phrases related to movement and dance commands, matching them to pictures.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a card (e.g., 'jump') and asks students to find the matching picture from a set of three pictures on their desk.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher displays several movement words (e.g., 'spin', 'shake', 'twirl') on the board. Students circle the word that matches the teacher's action. (Pronunciation focus: /t/ in 'two', /d/ in 'dance').</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Tracing letters and simple movement-related words (e.g., 'hop', 'run', 'step', 'jump', 'spin', 'dance'). Identifying initial letters ('J' for 'jump', 'S' for 'spin', 'T' for 'turn', 'V' in words later if applicable). Writing simple directive phrases as labels (e.g., 'Move up', 'Step fast').</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 1: Trace the Dance Words.</b> Students receive a worksheet with large, dotted outlines of target movement words (e.g., 'jump', 'hop', 'run', 'spin', 'step', 'dance'). Below each word, there is a simple drawing of the action. Students carefully trace each word using crayons or pencils. Teacher circulates, providing individual guidance on letter formation and reminding students of the sounds (e.g., "J-J-J-jump").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 2: Match and Label.</b> Students receive another worksheet with two columns. Column A has pictures of different movements (e.g., 'move to the front', 'stand next to the chair', 'jump over the line'). Column B has the corresponding words/phrases mixed up. Students draw a line from each picture to its correct word. Then, they trace the word they matched, next to the picture it describes. (Vocabulary: chair, line, next to, over, front, move, jump).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Activity 3: Draw and Fill-in.</b> Students get a worksheet with a few empty boxes. Below each box, there's a sentence with a missing word (e.g., "I can ____." "Let's ____ big!"). The missing words are chosen from a word bank (e.g., 'dance', 'step', 'shake'). Students draw a simple picture of the action in the box and then choose the correct word from the word bank to write (or trace from a model) in the blank. Peer check: In pairs, students show their drawings and words to each other. "Is this 'dance'?" they ask each other, using polite expressions like 'go ahead' to indicate turns.</li>
                    </ul>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Let's Dance!".</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "My Dance Move Book." Each student creates a mini-book (two folded sheets of paper) with 4 pages. On each page, they draw a simple picture of a dance move (e.g., 'spin', 'twirl', 'hop', 'jump'). Then, below their drawing, they attempt to write the word for the action. They can use the flashcards or previous worksheets as reference for spelling. Some pages might also include numbers (e.g., drawing two steps and writing 'two steps').</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> This activity directly encourages students to visualize and document dance moves from the "Let's Dance!" scenario within the "Move Around" theme by actively writing their labels.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in drawing simple actions and independently attempting to write (trace or copy) corresponding labels. Collaborative interaction as they show their "dance move book" to a partner at the end.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "My Dance Move Book" creation and a simple peer assessment during sharing time.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "My Dance Move Book" to a classmate. They point to a drawing and say the action, then point to the word they wrote. Their partner provides simple feedback (e.g., "Good drawing!" or "Yes, that's 'jump'!"). The teacher notes accuracy of labels and effort in writing.</p>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of labeled words (recognizing if the word matches the drawing).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legibility and effort in tracing/writing the words.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Engagement in the task and interaction with peer.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students hold up a "thumb up" if they can draw and label 2 dance moves, or a "thumb sideways" if they need more practice. Alternatively, they circle one of three faces on a small slip: a happy face for "I can write dance words!", a neutral face for "I can write some dance words," or a sad face for "I need help writing dance words."</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was your favorite dance move to draw and write today?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What word did you write all by yourself?"</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                    <ul style="margin-top: 4px; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in helping students trace and label movement words? The multi-sensory approach of drawing and then tracing seemed effective for Kinder.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face with writing? Some struggled with letter formation for words like 'twirl' or 'shake'. More tracing practice or simpler words might be needed next time for independent writing.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments/scaffolds needed: Provide more pre-written word banks for less confident writers, or allow more tracing templates for complex words. Integrate more phonemic awareness for letters S, T, V.</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> For Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their learning by creating a "Dance Instruction Poster" with pictures and their own labels/simple commands. This will integrate their drawing and writing skills to mediate simple instructions to an audience, culminating in a 21st Century Skills Project.</p>
                </td>
                <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                    <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>